use std::effects::exception::{ Exception }

## Ability for types that can be unwrapped-or-short-circuited via `.try {}`.
##
## Implementing `Tryable` lets a value be unwrapped by calling `it.try {}`
## inside a `perform ExceptionToResult` scope: `try` extracts the success
## value, or throws via `Exception.throw` on failure. The enclosing
## `ExceptionToResult` handler folds that throw back into an `Err`, so a
## sequence of `.try {}` calls reads top-to-bottom and short-circuits on the
## first failure.
##
## Standard impls live in `std::result` (for `Result 'a 'e`, throws the raw
## error) and `std::option` (for `Option 'a`, throws a synthetic `NoneOpt`
## variant). Custom failure types can implement `Tryable` to participate.
#{ missing: "`.try {}` unwraps `Option`, `Result`, or any type with an `impl Tryable`." }
ability Tryable for 'm having 'inner where {
  ## Extract the success value, throwing on failure.
  ##
  ## On success (`Ok {val}`, `Some {val}`, etc.), return the inner value.
  ## On failure, call `Exception.throw` with whatever value the surrounding
  ## `ExceptionToResult` handler should turn into `Err`. The receiver's
  ## evaluation can have any effect; those effects flow into the surrounding
  ## expression's effect row independently of `try`'s `<Exception>`.
  try: { it: 'm } -> 'inner <Exception>
}
